```ts
const TTL_MS = 60_000;
const cache = new Map<string, { expires: number; value: Promise<UserProfile> }>();

export function getUserProfile(id: string): Promise<UserProfile> {
  const hit = cache.get(id);
  if (hit && hit.expires > Date.now()) return hit.value;

  const value = fetchUserProfile(id);
  cache.set(id, { expires: Date.now() + TTL_MS, value });
  // Do not keep failed requests cached.
  value.catch(() => {
    if (cache.get(id)?.value === value) cache.delete(id);
  });
  return value;
}

export function invalidateUserProfile(id: string): void {
  cache.delete(id);
}
```

Call `invalidateUserProfile(id)` wherever a profile is updated, otherwise readers see stale data for up to 60 seconds. Caching the promise rather than the result means concurrent requests for the same user share one fetch.

Security warning: the cache is keyed by user ID only, so if the response differs depending on who is asking (for example, private fields shown only to the owner), add the viewer to the key or one viewer will be served another's version.

Left out:
- **Size cap:** entries are only replaced when the same ID is requested again, so add LRU eviction if the set of user IDs is large.
- **Shared store:** the cache is per process, so move it to Redis if you run more than one instance and need invalidation to reach all of them.
